26: Core Prompting Techniques for software testing

    1: Prompt Chaining: (Chain-of-thought)
          > Involves breaking a complex task into a series of intermediate steps (multiple prompts)
          > Each step's output is checked/refined before moving forward
          > This approach builds accuracy as each response informs the next prompt
          > Ideal for complicated test processes
          > It also allows for dynamic interactions in test processes
          Example:
            > Step 1: Ask LLM to generate test cases for login
            > Step 2: Refine cases to include edge scenarios (Ex : Empty password, SQL injection)
            > Step 3: Validate outputs against requirements and refine again if needed.
     
    2: Zero Shot Prmpting
         > Zero-Shot prompting relies entirely on the LLM's pre-existing knowledge
         > No examples are provided in the prompt
         > It is quick and simple prompting but may lack precision
         > Example:
              > Prompt : "Generate boundary test cases for the age field"
              > LLM users its own knowledge to produce cases (ex: negative values, large numbers, valid ranges)

    3: One Shot prompting
        > In One-SHot prompting you provide one example to demonstrate the desired outcome
        > It helps in guiding the LLM towards the expected format or behaviour
        > Example: 
            > Prompt: Generate boundary test cases in tabular fomrat for the age field with range 1 and 110. Ex: TCID, Age Value, Expected Result   TC-XX, -1, INVALID
            > LLm then products additional cases following the same style

    4: Few Shot prompting
        > In Few shot prompting you provide multiple examples in the prompt
        > It reinforces the expected behaviour and format
        > It helps the LLM to generalize more effectively
        > Example:
            > Prompt : Generate boundary test cases for the aga field with range 1 and 110. Ex: Age = -1 in Invalid, Age = 0 is Valid, Age = 120 is Invalid
            > LLM continues with consistent outputs (eg: Age = 25 is valid, Age = 130 is Invalid)

    5: Meta Prompting
        > In Meta prompting LLM helps generate or refine its own prompts
        > It follows interative cycle
            > If tester is unsure of prompt, in that case tester evaluates and LLM improves prompt, this cycle is repeated until best prompt is acchieved.
        > It reduces manual effort in designing effective prompts
            Ex: 
              > Tester: Suggest a better way to phrase this test prompt for login security
              > LLM: Refines prompt to include password complexity, lockout rules, error handling
              > Tester: Accepts or adjusts until desired outcome
        > Benefits of Meta Prompting
              > It enccourages collaborating (paring) between tester & AI
              >  It enhances productivity, learning and creativity
              > It is useful in pair programming / pain testing contexts
  
27: Hands on | Top Prompting Techniques

      1: Zero Shot Prompting : Direct instruction with no examples. Great for testing general capability
      2: One Shot Prmpting : One example is provided to guide the model's response format or tone
      3: Few Shot Prompting : Multiple examples are given to help the model learn a pattern or structure
      4: Chain of thought : Encourages the model to reason step-by-step before answering. Userful for complex tasks
      5: Compound prompting : Combines multiple prompt types for layered reasoning or multi-step tasks.

